This Element is not met as evidenced by:
Based on observation during one of three home visits (Patient (P) 2), medical record review, and review of facility policy and procedure, it was determined that the facility failed to ensure all patients participate in their pain assessment through the self-rating of pain in accordance with facility policy. 

Findings include:

Facility policy titled, "Pain Assessment and Management" (last review date 3/7/23) states, "...Pain Management and Quality Indicators 1. On a scale of one to ten or other measurable scale, record the patient's rating of their pain. …Document in the clinical record: General status of patient including...self-rating of the pain. …"

On 9/26/23 at 2:57 PM, during a home visit for P2, Staff (S)9 (Occupational Therapist) was observed instructing the patient to perform exercises. P2 reported left shoulder pain and stated, "Ouch." P2 continued to perform the exercises in the presence of S9, and S9 asked questions regarding P2's medications. While moving his/her arm, P2 stated, "It's cracking here. And the pain is going down to the elbows." S9 stated that he/she would inquire into the patient's recent orthopedic visit. S9 did not assess the patient's pain P2 to provide a self-rating of pain at the time of the visit, or the minimum or maximum amount of pain in the last five days, during the visit. 

On 9/28/23 at 9:15 AM, during a review of P2's medical record, the "Visit Note Report" documented by S9 revealed the following: "Vital Signs...Pain 0...Assessment...Pain...Indicate pain scale for patient ratings: Scale: 0-10 Indicate minimum amount of pain the patient has had in the last 5 days: 2  Indicate maximum amount of pain the patient has had in the last 5 days: 3...." The above findings were confirmed with S1 (Executive Director of Operations) at 12:20 PM. 

