This Element is not met as evidenced by:

Based on observation, medical record review, review of agency documents, and staff interview, it was determined that the facility failed to ensure the plan of care includes all components for individualized care to be provided to the patient and kept up to date. 

Findings include:

Reference #1: Facility policy titled "Monitoring Patient's Response/Reporting to Physician," states, "...POLICY Clinicians will monitor, document, and report the patient's response to care and treatment provided on each home visit,,,,, PROCEDURE 1. During each home visit, the clinician will monitor the patient's response to care against the established goals... 7. The plan of care will be updated according to the organization's policies and procedures....."

Reference #2: Facility policy titled "Home Health Aide Plan of Care," states, "... Procedure: ... 2. The home health aide plan of care will be individualized to the specific patient. The following will be included as appropriate: ... F. Activities permitted ..." 

1. During a home visit with Patient #13 on 11/8/18, the following was noted:

a. The patient was diagnosed with Intrahepatic Bile Duct Carcinoma, Malignant Ascites, and Cirrhosis of the Liver. The Plan of Care stated the following: 

(i) Drain 1000 mls (milliliters) from the Aspira Drainage System every Monday, Wednesday and Friday. 

(ii) Skilled nursing full systems assessment every visit. 

b. The Goals stated the patient was homebound due to shortness of breath, and required frequent rest, and was at risk for emergent care/re-hospitalization due to Ascites and Liver Carcinoma. 

c. There was no evidence in the plan of care, or at time of observation that the nurse assessed, discussed or educated the patient for increase or decrease of fluid retention secondary to the disease process. 

(i) There was no assessment or plan of care by Staff #12, to determine if the ascites was increasing or decreasing, effecting the quality of care or changes in patient care needs. 

2. On 11/9/18, Patient #16 was seen at a home visit by Staff #21, a nurse, and Staff #22, a home health aide. Medical record documents were reviewed and the following was identified:

a. On 9/18/18, the "Referral Profile" noted, "the patient underwent surgery and was placed in a cast and is to be non-weight bearing." Patient #16's start of care date (SoC) was 9/21/18.

b. Review of the home health aide care plan, that was in the home for the aide to follow, revealed the following: 

(i) The section: "Patient Activity - Weight Bearing" had no documentation. Under the section, "Ambulate Patient," "walker" was written. 

(ii) An interview conducted with Patient #16, in the presence of Staff #21, confirmed the patient had surgery on 9/15/18 and had been wheelchair bound "for awhile" and had been instructed to be non-weight bearing on the right foot.

(iii) On 11/9/18, during the home visit, Staff #21 updated the care plan to reflect "Wheelchair Bound" next the written "walker" for the aide to follow. 

(iv) There were no updated instructions provided to the aide by the nurse to ensure that the patient remained non-weight bearing and the update was a late revision to the care plan from the SoC on 9/21/18.

3. Staff #3, Staff #4, and Staff #10 confirmed the above findings.

