This Element is not met as evidenced by:
Based on staff interview, review of facility documents, and medical record review, it was determined that the facility failed to ensure that patient's receive the home health services that are established in the plan of care outlined by the physician.

Findings include:

Reference: Document titled, "Visiting Nurse Associations of America 19th Edition Clinical Procedure Manual," states, "...Nutrition - ASSESSMENT: NUTRITION KEY POINTS 1. Nutrition screening and assessment is an essential element of home health practice..... 5. ...Consider a referral in the following situations: ...b. Nutritional interventions need to be determined for high risk patients..... AFTERCARE 1. Communicate with the physician about: a. Recommended diet b. Need for referral to Registered Dietitian Nutritionist... 3. Document: ...c. Instruction provided to patient/caregiver about diet and nutrition d. Communication with physician or team members... "

1. On 2/4/19, the medical record of Patient #4 was reviewed. The HEALTH CERTIFICATION AND PLAN OF CARE, that was received as a verbal start of care (SOC for 12/1/18), noted, "...Orders of Discipline and Treatments: Skilled nurse to evaluate and develop plan of care in consultation with the physician that includes... health promotion, and nutritional needs. Skilled nurse to assess/evaluate co-morbid conditions... to identify changes and intervene to minimize complications. Perform coordination of care with internal and external teams members..."

a. The OASIS admission assessment was completed on 12/1/18 by Staff #22, a registered nurse (RN), that identified Patient #4 had an unintentional weight loss of a total of 10 pounds or more in the past 12 months. 

(i) Patient #4's weight was recorded by Staff #22 on 12/1/18 as 108 pounds.

(ii) Staff #22 noted in the "Nutritional" section of the OASIS assessment that Patient #4 had a change in the ability to access and prepare meals, and was indicated to present as a high nutritional risk.

b. Staff #22 documented that instructions were provided to the patient/caregiver regarding nutritional/hydration requirements of diet prescribed by the physician on the OASIS admission assessment dated 12/1/18.

(i) Patient #4 was ordered to have a "Regular" diet.

c. Staff #22 documented that the patient/caregiver verbalized understanding of basic nutritional/hydration requirements as a "met goal" on the OASIS admission assessment dated 12/1/18.        

2. Patient #4 was admitted to the hospital on 12/26/18, and had a resumption of care (ROC) with the home health agency (HHA) on 12/29/18.

a. Staff #23 performed a ROC assessment on 12/29/18, which identified Patient #4's weight as 100 pounds. This was a weight loss of 8 pounds in 28 days.

b. Staff #23 noted in the "Nutritional" section of the ROC assessment that Patient #4 was currently experiencing nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, or constipation.

c. Staff #23 indicated that the patient did not have an unintentional weight loss in the past 6 months and that the patient presented at a moderate nutritional risk.

3. The medical record of Patient #4 lacked evidence that the nutritional status of the patient was discussed between the nurse and the physician. 

4. An interview with Staff #1 on 2/4/19 at 2:02 PM confirmed that the medical record of Patient #4 lacked evidence of a referral or an offer to be referred to a registered dietitian nutritionist.

a. Staff #1 confirmed on 2/4/19 at 2:02 PM that Patient #4 would have been a candidate for a referral to a registered dietitian nutritionist.

